32
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:05,570
so here we have a brief
session outline but overall

33
00:02:05,570 --> 00:02:08,110
what we'll be covering is
autoimmune diseases which

34
00:02:08,110 --> 00:02:11,610
kind of have a central focus
in the mechanisms of what

35
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:14,470
they're going to be
presenting with so we have our

36
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:17,550
own actual immune system and
not going into the details

37
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:21,210
of innate versus adaptive
immunity anytime our body

38
00:02:21,210 --> 00:02:23,690
in general comes across
an antigen that it doesn't

39
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:27,370
recognize or doesn't know,
we are activating our immune

40
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:30,690
system to try to attack
it. And so what happens in

41
00:02:30,690 --> 00:02:33,550
this case is we have an
immune response to our self

42
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:36,050
-antigens, and that's what
leads to this autoimmunity.

43
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:39,250
There is a self-tolerance
mechanism where we're able

44
00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:41,890
to recognize our own
antigens. However, in these

45
00:02:41,890 --> 00:02:45,070
autoimmune processes, this
fails, and this leads to

46
00:02:45,070 --> 00:02:49,410
autoimmune antibodies and
also autoreactive T cells and

47
00:02:49,410 --> 00:02:52,770
overall just an inappropriate
immune response. And so

48
00:02:52,770 --> 00:02:55,550
what we'll be covering is
diseases that not only affect

49
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:58,530
just the skin, like Sjogren's
syndrome and Pumphagus

50
00:02:58,530 --> 00:03:01,690
vulgaris, but anywhere
along the way of the GI

51
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:05,510
tract, such as the IBD symptoms
and syndromes, and also

52
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:08,430
the thyroid glands, to
disease processes that you'll

53
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:11,150
see day-to-day, such as
insulin-dependent diabetes.

54
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:18,090
We'll be starting our
discussion with autoimmune

55
00:03:18,090 --> 00:03:22,170
diseases of the endocrine
and exocrine glands. One way

56
00:03:22,170 --> 00:03:24,770
that I like to break down
in my head the distinction

57
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:28,210
between endocrine versus
exocrine glands, and this may

58
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:30,510
help in understanding some
of the diseases we'll be

59
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:34,470
talking about today, is
consider endocrine to be when

60
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:37,330
our body produces something
like a hormone and it's

61
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:40,110
secreted systemically throughout
your body whether you're

62
00:03:40,110 --> 00:03:43,670
using arterial or venous
systems. However, when

63
00:03:43,670 --> 00:03:46,570
you're looking at exocrine
glands, think of products

64
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:49,850
being produced by your body
in one specific organ or

65
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:52,990
one specific area to be
presented on the surface of

66
00:03:52,990 --> 00:03:56,810
another. The easiest examples
are salivary glands producing

67
00:03:56,810 --> 00:04:00,330
saliva or lacrimal glands
presenting with tears.

68
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:08,030
Our first disease we'll be
talking about is Hashimoto's

69
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:11,110
thyroiditis, which is
actually the most common

70
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:15,730
cause of hypothyroidism in
areas that are not resource

71
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:18,870
scarce or limited in
iodine. So you'll see this

72
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:23,110
commonly presented in the
setting of hypothyroidism. In

73
00:04:23,110 --> 00:04:25,890
this case, the concern is
that there are antibodies

74
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:29,650
to thyroid peroxidase enzyme,
which you'll see centrally

75
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:32,730
located in this picture.
And what happens is this

76
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:35,890
enzyme in the thyroid is
actually there to oxidize

77
00:04:35,890 --> 00:04:39,470
the iodide ions which
catalyze the formation of your

78
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:43,650
T4 and T3 hormones and so
when you have these antibodies

79
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:47,330
you'll see downstream
effects of not having enough

80
00:04:47,330 --> 00:04:51,490
T3 and T4 ultimately
causing your hypothyroidism.

81
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:58,470
For Hashimoto's thyroiditis
the mechanism of action

82
00:04:58,470 --> 00:05:02,390
is actually IgG mediated
autoantibody production

83
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:05,590
against thyroid peroxidase,
which is the enzyme

84
00:05:05,590 --> 00:05:08,190
we mentioned on the
previous screen, as well as

85
00:05:08,190 --> 00:05:11,530
thyroglobulin. And this
is organ-specific, meaning

86
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:14,710
we're specifically dealing
with infiltration of the

87
00:05:14,710 --> 00:05:16,930
thyroid gland. However,
the signs and symptoms

88
00:05:16,930 --> 00:05:19,270
that you see are going
to be systemic throughout

89
00:05:19,270 --> 00:05:23,010
the body. This is a type 2
hypersensitivity reaction

90
00:05:23,010 --> 00:05:25,690
because we have specific
antibodies targeting

91
00:05:25,690 --> 00:05:29,350
endogenous cells resulting
in ultimate destruction.

92
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:33,050
In terms of signs and
symptoms and some of the risk

93
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:36,510
factors, there are environmental
risk factors such as

94
00:05:36,510 --> 00:05:39,330
smoking and drugs as well
as genetic causes such as

95
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:44,370
HLA or human leukocyte
antigen DR5 and DR3. And what

96
00:05:44,370 --> 00:05:47,130
you'll see on presentation is
someone will have a diffuse

97
00:05:47,130 --> 00:05:50,950
and non-tender large
thyroid or thyroid a megaly.

98
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:53,590
Furthermore, you'll see
signs and symptoms of

99
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:55,600
hypothyroidism. People
will complain about

100
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:59,590
constipation, weight gain,
frail hair, and other

101
00:05:59,590 --> 00:06:01,890
signs and stigmata of
hypothyroid disease.

102
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:07,710
In terms of the diagnosis
of Hashimoto's thyroiditis,

103
00:06:07,710 --> 00:06:10,070
one of the tests that
we can use is anti

104
00:06:10,070 --> 00:06:13,610
-TPO antibodies against the
thyroid peroxidase enzyme,

105
00:06:13,610 --> 00:06:15,970
and this will actually
be present in about 90

106
00:06:15,970 --> 00:06:18,990
% of cases that we're
dealing with. Furthermore,

107
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:21,990
you can also test for
antithyroglobulin antibodies

108
00:06:21,990 --> 00:06:25,110
which will be present
in about 40% of cases.

109
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:28,630
On biopsy, one of the
things that you would find

110
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:31,390
is follicular damage with
chronic inflammation.

111
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:34,710
There's also this noted
finding of herthal cells

112
00:06:34,710 --> 00:06:37,210
and really what herthal
cells will distribute and

113
00:06:37,210 --> 00:06:40,450
demonstrate to you are
eosinophilic metaplasia.

114
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:42,750
In terms of the treatment,

115
00:06:42,930 --> 00:06:45,650
ultimately what this leads
to is hypothyroidism,

116
00:06:45,670 --> 00:06:48,260
and what we'll use for
treatment is levothyroxine,

117
00:06:48,290 --> 00:06:51,610
which is really just a
synthetic version of T3 since

118
00:06:51,610 --> 00:06:54,150
the thyroid is no longer
able to generate this.

119
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:57,690
One thing that's very
interesting is that originally

120
00:06:57,690 --> 00:06:59,810
in Hashimoto's
thyroiditis, towards the

121
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:03,330
earlier phases, you can
actually see signs and symptoms

122
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:06,070
of hyperthyroidism,
meaning too much thyroid

123
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:08,650
hormone because there's
leaking of the hormones

124
00:07:08,650 --> 00:07:11,530
from follicular damage.
However, ultimately this

125
00:07:11,530 --> 00:07:14,290
will end up leading to
your hypothyroid state.

126
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:20,510
Now we'll be briefly talking
about insulin-dependent

127
00:07:20,510 --> 00:07:23,410
diabetes mellitus, and in
this case we're talking about

128
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:27,090
a type 4 hypersensitivity
reaction with involvement

129
00:07:27,090 --> 00:07:30,590
of T cells as well as
macrophagic infiltration into

130
00:07:30,590 --> 00:07:33,970
the beta cells of the
pancreas. So this is organ

131
00:07:33,970 --> 00:07:36,680
-specific, we're dealing with
the pancreatic beta cells

132
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:39,930
whose role is to produce
insulin, and what we'll see is

133
00:07:39,930 --> 00:07:43,110
not only decreased insulin,
but systemic signs and

134
00:07:43,110 --> 00:07:46,550
symptoms throughout the body
of the sequelae of not having

135
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:49,990
enough insulin. There are
some genetic predispositions

136
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:54,190
and associations,
specifically with HLA-DR3 and

137
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:58,190
DR4, and until this hits a
tipping point, it's often

138
00:07:58,190 --> 00:08:02,170
asymptomatic, but very, very
commonly seen, very commonly

139
00:08:02,170 --> 00:08:05,130
tested in preclinical and
clinical conditions and

140
00:08:05,130 --> 00:08:09,350
situations. You'll have DKA
or diabetic ketoacidosis

141
00:08:09,350 --> 00:08:12,470
as a common initial
presentation. In this case,

142
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:15,890
patients are often tested
and seen with a high anti-gap

143
00:08:15,890 --> 00:08:19,770
metabolic acidosis and signs
and symptoms of polyuria

144
00:08:19,770 --> 00:08:23,630
and polydipsia, having excessive
thirst and also urinating

145
00:08:23,630 --> 00:08:26,390
quite frequently, and
having not only ketones in

146
00:08:26,390 --> 00:08:29,370
the urine but also glucose.
Your body does its best

147
00:08:29,370 --> 00:08:32,250
to maintain a certain level
of glucose with your kidneys.

148
00:08:32,250 --> 00:08:34,730
However, what happens is
we do urinate out some

149
00:08:34,730 --> 00:08:38,470
of this, and with that comes
osmotic diuresis, glucose,

150
00:08:38,470 --> 00:08:41,870
and water, leading to
overall a dehydrated picture.

151
00:08:43,970 --> 00:08:47,010
In terms of how we test
for insulin-dependent

152
00:08:47,010 --> 00:08:49,870
diabetes mellitus, one
of the most common ways

153
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:52,550
is with oral blood glucose
testing. So there's

154
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:55,090
actually parameters set
for what a fasting sugar

155
00:08:55,090 --> 00:08:57,840
or glucose should be
as well as non-fasting.

156
00:08:58,110 --> 00:09:01,020
Furthermore, we also look at
what's called the hemoglobin

157
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:04,950
A1c above the range of 6
.5. So you'll learn about

158
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:09,270
pre-diabetic ranges and
diabetic ranges, 6.5 pertaining

159
00:09:09,270 --> 00:09:12,410
to the diabetic range. And
what this test is, is you're

160
00:09:12,410 --> 00:09:14,930
actually looking at your
hemoglobin, which has about

161
00:09:14,930 --> 00:09:18,210
a 90-day lifespan. And with
that, we're looking at the

162
00:09:18,210 --> 00:09:21,170
glycated versions. So
there's actually a part or a

163
00:09:21,170 --> 00:09:24,670
certain amount of your hemoglobin
that has sugar or glucose

164
00:09:24,670 --> 00:09:27,710
molecules attached to it,
calling it glycated, and

165
00:09:27,710 --> 00:09:30,520
the percentage of this is
how you determine your A1c.

166
00:09:30,670 --> 00:09:32,670
It's actually a
three-month average,

167
00:09:32,670 --> 00:09:34,570
and that's actually
referring back to

168
00:09:34,570 --> 00:09:37,230
your RBC, your red
blood cell survival.

169
00:09:37,470 --> 00:09:40,630
There are a few antibodies
that you can see in

170
00:09:40,630 --> 00:09:43,670
this disease process,
specifically the anti-glutamic

171
00:09:43,670 --> 00:09:46,470
acid, as well as the
islet cell cytoplasmic

172
00:09:46,470 --> 00:09:49,650
antibodies. However, these
antibodies don't play

173
00:09:49,650 --> 00:09:52,170
a role in the pathology
like we talked about with

174
00:09:52,170 --> 00:09:54,570
Hashimoto's. They just
seem to be present in

175
00:09:54,570 --> 00:09:57,130
the individuals with
insulin-dependent diabetes.

176
00:09:57,750 --> 00:10:00,850
The treatment mainly
is exogenous insulin

177
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:03,150
or ultimately a
pancreas transplant.

178
00:10:03,190 --> 00:10:07,270
A lot of times we also
specify type 1 diabetes versus

179
00:10:07,270 --> 00:10:11,030
type 2 diabetes, referring
to type 1 as the autoimmune

180
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:14,070
variant. However, there's
also a spectrum of diseases

181
00:10:14,070 --> 00:10:17,470
specifically with one called
latent autoimmune disease

182
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:20,730
of adult or latent autoimmune
diabetes in adults,

183
00:10:20,730 --> 00:10:23,700
in which case you'll also
have a similar pathophysiology

184
00:10:23,930 --> 00:10:27,610
and pathogenesis with an
autoimmune presentation.

185
00:10:31,510 --> 00:10:34,630
Next, we'll be briefly
discussing Sjogren's syndrome,

186
00:10:34,630 --> 00:10:37,170
in which we have a
mechanism of lymphocytic

187
00:10:37,170 --> 00:10:40,360
infiltration as well as
fibrosis leading to destruction,

188
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:43,210
and in this case it's
primary or secondary,

189
00:10:43,210 --> 00:10:46,010
meaning Sjogren's syndrome
can stand on its own as a

190
00:10:46,010 --> 00:10:48,830
disease process, typically
known as Sikha syndrome,

191
00:10:48,830 --> 00:10:51,350
as mentioned below, or
it's actually a sign and

192
00:10:51,350 --> 00:10:54,030
symptom along with
another form of autoimmune

193
00:10:54,030 --> 00:10:57,410
condition, such as rheumatoid
arthritis, Hashimoto's,

194
00:10:57,530 --> 00:11:01,670
systemic lupus. And this is
another good example, just

195
00:11:01,670 --> 00:11:04,590
like we mentioned with diabetes,
of type 4 hypersensitivity

196
00:11:04,590 --> 00:11:07,570
reaction, because there's
no actual antibody

197
00:11:07,570 --> 00:11:10,930
involvement in the autoimmune
destruction. So in these

198
00:11:10,930 --> 00:11:13,690
patients, what you'll see
is it's not organ-specific,

199
00:11:13,690 --> 00:11:17,150
but it is typically in the
exocrine glands, especially

200
00:11:17,150 --> 00:11:20,010
with the lacrimal and
salivary glands. And so what

201
00:11:20,010 --> 00:11:24,190
you'll see is xerostomia,
which is dry mouth due to

202
00:11:24,190 --> 00:11:27,250
decreased saliva production.
This actually ultimately

203
00:11:27,250 --> 00:11:30,430
leads to mucosal atrophy,
you'll see fissuring of the

204
00:11:30,430 --> 00:11:33,690
tongue, and there's also this
problem of recurrent dental

205
00:11:33,690 --> 00:11:36,490
caries because of the
decreased saliva production.

206
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:39,050
Furthermore, you also have

207
00:11:39,340 --> 00:11:41,710
keratoconjunctivitis, so
this is dry eyes because

208
00:11:41,710 --> 00:11:43,610
of a decrease in
tear production,

209
00:11:43,810 --> 00:11:47,050
which leads to a higher
risk of corneal damage.

210
00:11:47,390 --> 00:11:49,750
Furthermore, you'll
also see parotid gland

211
00:11:49,750 --> 00:11:52,250
***********, so two of the
salivary glands on either

212
00:11:52,250 --> 00:11:54,670
side of the face, and
the *********** is either

213
00:11:54,670 --> 00:11:57,590
a compensatory mechanism
or because of fatty

214
00:11:57,590 --> 00:11:59,930
tissue replacement,
and ultimately there's

215
00:11:59,930 --> 00:12:03,230
actually a higher risk
for cancer, marginal zone

216
00:12:03,230 --> 00:12:06,690
lymphomas, and mucosa
-associated lymphoid tissue.

217
00:12:10,050 --> 00:12:13,590
In regards to the diagnosis
of Sjogren's syndrome,

218
00:12:13,590 --> 00:12:17,170
what we'll find is about
in 70% of cases, we have a

219
00:12:17,170 --> 00:12:20,590
positive rheumatoid factor.
However, this is not specific

220
00:12:20,590 --> 00:12:23,930
or sensitive to Sjogren's
syndrome, however a common

221
00:12:23,930 --> 00:12:27,430
finding throughout autoimmune
conditions. Furthermore,

222
00:12:27,430 --> 00:12:30,670
we'll have a positive
ANA, and if we were to do

223
00:12:31,190 --> 00:12:33,550
immunofluorescence, what
we'll actually see is

224
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:36,670
nuclear speckling, and
that's shown to you in the

225
00:12:36,670 --> 00:12:39,290
third picture on the right
-hand side of the page.

226
00:12:39,530 --> 00:12:42,170
Furthermore, there are
some antibodies that we can

227
00:12:42,170 --> 00:12:45,190
test for, and in this case
specifically, we can look

228
00:12:45,190 --> 00:12:48,270
at anti-rho antibodies,
which stands for anti

229
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:53,070
-ribonucleoprotein antibodies,
as well as anti-LAW or SSB.

230
00:12:53,750 --> 00:12:56,990
Clinically, we can also
do a Shermer test, which

231
00:12:56,990 --> 00:12:59,090
is really there to just
tell us if there's enough

232
00:12:59,090 --> 00:13:02,010
tears being produced by
the lacrimal glands, and

233
00:13:02,010 --> 00:13:05,450
there's also a predominance
or focus on biopsies.

234
00:13:05,450 --> 00:13:07,790
So if we were to get a
diagnosis, we might do a

235
00:13:07,790 --> 00:13:10,850
lip biopsy, in which case
we'll see lymphoid foci

236
00:13:10,850 --> 00:13:14,810
or perioductal lymphocytic
